---
description: Supermemory persistent memory tools — use them proactively
alwaysApply: true
---

You have access to Supermemory MCP tools for persistent memory across coding sessions. At the start of every substantive turn, decide whether prior work, decisions, preferences, bugs, or repository conventions could help. If so, call `search_memory` with the user's request before doing the work. Skip recall for greetings, acknowledgements, and unrelated one-line requests.

- `search_memory`: Search before answering questions about previous work, bugs, or patterns. The result also carries the container's profile summary.
- `add_memory`: Save decisions, fixes, and preferences when the user asks or when they are clearly valuable. Use its `forget` mode to remove outdated memories.
- `listMemories`: List recent memories for this project, with their IDs.
- `listSpaces` / `whoAmI`: Resolve a space the user names, or report which account and space are active.

Pass `containerTag` on every call, using the container tag from the
`<supermemory-context>` block injected at session start. Without it the hosted
server writes to the account's active space, where this project's session
recall will not find the memory.

When to use:
- User mentions past sessions, previous work, or "last time" → search first
- User says "remember", "save this", "don't forget" → add immediately
- After solving a hard bug → save the solution and root cause
- Discovering architectural patterns or conventions → save them
- User asks why something was done a certain way → search for context

Memories from Supermemory are marked ◪, both in injected context and in `search_memory`, `get_profile` and `list_memories` results. When one shapes your answer, credit it naturally with the ◪ prefix (e.g. "◪ earlier you decided X"); if you name the source, say "from supermemory", never generic memory.
